WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:05.540
This last section will wrap up the Source Control and Collaboration course with a few

2
00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:10.419
more final recommendations, providing some tips and resources for you to use as you

3
00:00:10.419 --> 00:00:14.189
work in your collaborative production pipeline.

4
00:00:14.189 --> 00:00:16.230
Starting out, a big one.

5
00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:21.010
Changing file folder names, or as we like to call it, breaking Perforce.

6
00:00:21.010 --> 00:00:26.829
When working with Unreal Engine and Perforce, absolutely avoid renaming whenever possible.

7
00:00:26.829 --> 00:00:31.829
Raining can cause duplication of folders or synchronization disconnect with your data.

8
00:00:31.829 --> 00:00:36.750
Similar issues may arise if you disconnect from version control, rename, and then try

9
00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:39.189
reconnecting your files to Perforce.

10
00:00:39.189 --> 00:00:45.729
Next, we talked about adding files in previous sections of this course, but I want to reiterate,

11
00:00:45.729 --> 00:00:50.869
when marking your files or folders for add, make sure that you select the nested subfolders

12
00:00:50.869 --> 00:00:52.829
for good measure.

13
00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:57.789
This will help Perforce isolate your files and only selecting the parent folder can run

14
00:00:57.789 --> 00:01:00.509
the risk of generating fractured data.

15
00:01:00.509 --> 00:01:05.590
With that said, Perforce has gotten better at the marking for add function in newer

16
00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.629
versions, so if you forget to do this, don't worry too much about it.

17
00:01:09.629 --> 00:01:13.590
Just try and make a habit of doing it before marking for add and submitting.

18
00:01:13.590 --> 00:01:18.579
Now, if your data is not syncing, one of the things we recommend is creating a fresh

19
00:01:18.579 --> 00:01:22.079
pull of information that should correct this problem.

20
00:01:22.079 --> 00:01:26.760
To do that, simply right-click on the file or folder in Perforce that is causing you

21
00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:28.420
problems.

22
00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:34.260
Select the Get Revision option to open the Get Revision pop-up menu.

23
00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:40.099
Once open, choose the Force Operation option and click Get Revision.

24
00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:44.799
That will force a fresh pull of all selected data, but be aware that this process will

25
00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:49.640
take some time to replace all those files, so make sure to take a short coffee break

26
00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:51.670
once you start the pull.

27
00:01:51.750 --> 00:01:57.700
Now, let's talk about Perforce and World Partition, and this first recommendation

28
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.819
is to employ data layers when using World Partition.

29
00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:06.060
Spotable actors may also be an option when working with data layers that become

30
00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:09.300
difficult to manage early on in your production.

31
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:12.349
Keep an eye out for when that happens.

32
00:02:12.349 --> 00:02:16.770
It is also highly recommended that you implement one file per actor, and the reason for

33
00:02:16.770 --> 00:02:21.669
that is because one file per actor will provide you with a hash code that is searchable

34
00:02:21.669 --> 00:02:23.469
in Perforce.

35
00:02:23.469 --> 00:02:28.349
That way, assets created for World Partition in Unreal Engine can then be searched for

36
00:02:28.349 --> 00:02:31.509
in Perforce with that code.

37
00:02:31.509 --> 00:02:35.189
Continuing the discussion about Perforce and World Partition, let's focus a little

38
00:02:35.189 --> 00:02:40.189
more on one file per actor, which can help ensure a file is checked out when adding

39
00:02:40.189 --> 00:02:42.539
it to the World Partition.

40
00:02:42.539 --> 00:02:47.159
Do not leave the World Partition checked out for too long, as this may cause problems

41
00:02:47.159 --> 00:02:48.819
for other users.

42
00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:52.050
Be sure to check in the World Partition when you are finished.

43
00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:57.650
Now, there are two items that are needed for tracking with one file per actor.

44
00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:01.469
As we just mentioned, the package short name, which is the searchable actor hash

45
00:03:01.469 --> 00:03:06.340
code for Perforce, and then the revision control icon.

46
00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:11.240
As you can see in this image, the revision control displays your asset status, and in

47
00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:15.780
this example, that's the question mark, which means that the World Partition asset

48
00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.740
is not checked in.

49
00:03:17.740 --> 00:03:23.060
One thing to note here is that the revision control icon is vital when working on a team,

50
00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:27.780
and if it's not active, other users may not see what is checked out, and that can

51
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:32.879
prevent project validations, which is why Epic Games permanently enables this option

52
00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:34.020
in production.

53
00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:39.060
Now, if you do run into validation errors, open up a blank level and run the validation

54
00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:40.620
process again.

55
00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:45.060
This usually restores the validation due to a dirty save that's being done by Unreal

56
00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.500
Engine.

57
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:51.180
Doing this by closing the World Partition level is currently the only workaround for

58
00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.800
this problem.

59
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.759
And one final thing to note here.

60
00:03:54.759 --> 00:03:59.759
One file per actor is enabled by default within the World Partition, and currently

61
00:03:59.759 --> 00:04:03.680
experimental when being used outside of the World Partition.

62
00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:08.460
Now, this course really covered a lot of material, but don't worry, there's more

63
00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:10.139
help out there.

64
00:04:10.139 --> 00:04:13.439
The video links here are amazing resources.

65
00:04:13.439 --> 00:04:18.360
The first YouTube link is a two-hour live stream covering source control in depth, looking

66
00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:24.279
well beyond the initial commits and looking at scaling source control for a project.

67
00:04:24.279 --> 00:04:28.519
The next YouTube link is a source control webinar, looking at each of the source control

68
00:04:28.519 --> 00:04:33.459
options one by one and reviewing some of their technical and usability concerns in

69
00:04:33.459 --> 00:04:35.329
greater detail.

70
00:04:35.329 --> 00:04:40.050
Of course, we have our own documentation from Epic Games that are specific to using

71
00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:43.209
Perforce and source control with Unreal Engine.

72
00:04:43.209 --> 00:04:47.889
And if you're looking for a more Perforce specific document, then Perforce also has

73
00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:52.290
a curated page with all their collected Unreal Engine resources.

74
00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:55.689
A shout out to Sean Spitzer and Ryan Manning for their support in this course

75
00:04:55.689 --> 00:05:00.250
development, and this concludes the Source Control and Collaboration course.

76
00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:03.889
I want to thank you for spending your time working through this course material.
